{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on the source of the standing for Explore (request\n`72ff1c47`, section 5.7 of the act environment note): the session\nanswer first. The membership replica waits.\n\nWhy the session answer.\n- One reader. `issueSession` already runs `standingOf` at the\n  membership head (`packages/scope/src/sessions.ts:389`). The page,\n  the MCP server and the command line each hold a session. So no\n  client holds a role table, and `standingIn` of\n  `packages/mcp/src/inventory.ts:100` can go. That removes the\n  missing controller check of your answer d.\n- A bound. The value is one observation. A membership replica holds\n  a whole journal with no cap on memory, to learn the role of one\n  key (your answers a and c).\n- The cost is stated and small: the value is the statement of the\n  service at one membership head, no journal checks it, and it is\n  up to 600 seconds old. An exploration is a forecast. The scope\n  judges each submit with its own read, so an old value costs a\n  refused submit and never a wrong record.\n\nRules.\n1. The context names its standing: the observation, the membership\n   head that it was read at, and the fact that a session gave it.\n   Two explorations under equal contexts are equal. A later replica\n   can give the same shape with another source name.\n2. A row of `observes` stays `unavailable`. The value is no proof\n   of freshness and no grant for the runtime.\n3. A cached session with no observation gives no standing, and the\n   exploration is `unavailable`, as today.\n4. The standing changes when the instance receives a new session.\n   A refusal of a submit by the scope with an authority reason ends\n   the cached session, so the next read gets a new one. Silence\n   proves nothing, and the note says so.\n5. A reader checks what your answer e lists: the value fields, the\n   native pin, and that the key, the member, the membership and its\n   incarnation equal those of the request and of the claims.\n\nThree facts that I need before any source, in one act:\na. `isSessionAnswer` requires exactly three members. A deployed\n   page or a shipped command line at an older head would refuse an\n   answer with a fourth. Propose the route that keeps them working.\n   I expect that the request asks for the observation, and only\n   then the answer holds it. Say what an older Worker answers to\n   such a request.\nb. Does the contract already export a type that holds this\n   observation? A new export of `packages/contract` adds to the\n   kernel (`docs/kernel.md` section 7), and then I take it to the\n   owner with the review. Say which it is.\nc. The bytes of the observation for the three roles of Grow, as\n   the codec would send them, against the 16 KiB cap of the session\n   reply.\n\nE2 and E3 stay held until I rule on a to c. EK-1 does not wait for\nthis.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}